<title>JAPAN: Defence policymakers say more communication needed.</title>
<headline>Defence policymakers say more communication needed.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-10-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Defence policy officials from 19 countries in the Asia-Pacific region and the European Union ended two days of talks on Wednesday calling for greater communication on security issues.</p>
<p>Senior civil servants and military officers in charge of drafting defence policies from Thailand, Vietnam, Philippines, Malaysia, Singapore, Indonesia, Cambodia, Laos, Papua New Guinea, China, South Korea, Japan, Australia, New Zealand, Russia, Burma, India, Canada and the United States took part in the meetings in Tokyo.</p>
<p>Those invited included members of the ASEAN (Association of Southeast Asian Nations) Regional Forum, the only regional conference dedicated to security issues. North Korea was not invited since it is not a member of the forum.</p>
<p>&quot;There was a trend to seek increased transparency in defence policies, such as issuing white papers,&quot; Ryuichiro Yamazaki, the Japanese defence ministry's Director-General of International Affairs, told a news conference after the talks.</p>
<p>Yamazaki, who chaired the meeting, said a common headache among policy drafters was a lack of government funds in the post-Cold War era. Other issues included means to combat cross-border crimes, piracy and drug cartels.</p>
<p>While many countries appreciated the military presence of the United States, some countries were in principle opposed to keeping foreign troops on their soil, he said.</p>
<p>&quot;The attendants concluded that the next round should focus on specific areas, such as the relation of sub-regional or bilateral alliances to regional security,&quot; Yamazaki said.</p>
